  - [38;5;153mapp.js[39m — browser login form, runs in the user's page. Has [38;5;153mconsole.log("Logging in:", username)[39m and a
    [38;5;153mconsole.log("Login result:", result)[39m.
  - [38;5;153msrc/index.js[39m + [38;5;153msrc/utils.js[39m — a Node CLI entry point ([38;5;153mmain[39m in package.json), trivially small.
  - [38;5;153mpackage.json[39m — no dependencies, no scripts, no test runner. No linting, no tests, no CI.

  One thing I'll flag now rather than bury: [38;5;153mapp.js:5[39m logs a username on every login attempt, and the same handler has
  the password in scope. Whatever we build, "don't log credentials or PII" needs to be a rule the design enforces, not a
  habit we rely on — that's exactly the kind of thing that turns a debugging aid into a production incident.

  First question, since it changes nearly everything downstream:

[38;5;246m⏺ [39mUser answered Claude's questions:
[38;5;246m  ⎿  · Which app needs the logging? → Both, one shared module[39m

[38;5;231m⏺[39m Shared module it is — that's the heavier road (two runtimes, two transports), and it's the right call if both entry
  points are real production surfaces.

  Next question, because it can collapse or expand the whole design:
